course: dictionary
lesson: 13927
title: Legislation: laws made official
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Legislation is a law, or a set of laws, made by a parliament or government. It can also mean the process of making laws. Look at the words around it for clues. «Parliament passed new legislation to keep children safe near busy roads.» Passed and new tell you that something was agreed and made official.
explain: Try the swap check: if you put «laws» in the place of legislation and the sentence still makes sense, you have the right meaning. «The government introduced legislation to clean up rivers.» Introduced means put forward, so legislation here is a set of laws that has been proposed.
explain: Legislation is a big word for a big idea: a rule that everyone must follow, because it has been made official by the people in charge of making laws. When you see it in the news, it tells you the change is not just an idea. It is official.
fact: Legislation means a law or a set of laws made by a parliament or government.
fact: Legislation can also mean the process of making laws.
fact: Once legislation is passed, everyone must follow it, not just the people who wanted it.
fact: «Legislate» is the verb: it means to make laws.
fact: «The new legislation on school safety» means a law that makes schools safer for children.
exercise: «Parliament passed new legislation on school safety.» What does legislation mean here? | *A law made by parliament | A type of school lunch | A game played in class | A letter from a friend
exercise: «The government introduced legislation to protect the environment.» Find and copy the word that tells you the laws were put forward. | protect | environment | *introduced | government
exercise: «The new legislation came into force in May.» Which word is the best swap for legislation? | lake | *law | lamp | lesson
test: What is legislation? | *A law, or a set of laws, made by a parliament or government | A name for a shop that sells books | A type of school trip | A kind of weather that happens in spring
test: «Mia read about the legislation in the newspaper.» Which swap keeps the meaning best? | The new songs | The new lunches | *The new laws | The new games
exam: Which sentence uses legislation correctly? | I ate some legislation for lunch. | The legislation was a lovely pink colour. | *The government passed legislation to make seat belts compulsory. | We played legislation in the park after school.
